agents/counselors/1788309167-review-request-question-review-build-st/prompt.md:21:- Your previous design review (find the directory by grepping agents/counselors/*/prompt.md for "build step 8")
docs/phase-1-plan.md:18:8. **MCP auth:** bearer header first (see §3 "Connector auth"); the OAuth path is a separate step only if the Claude connector dialog offers no request-headers section.
docs/phase-1-plan.md:98:## 3. Tool surface (one registry, two transports)
docs/phase-1-plan.md:100:`app/models/agent/tools/*` each defines `name`, `description`, `input_schema`, `call(**args)`, and permission metadata: `read_only`, `interactive_allowed`, `scheduled_allowed`. The MCP server registers tools where `interactive_allowed`; the scheduled runtime passes only tools where `scheduled_allowed && read_only`, enforced in one place so a Phase 2 write tool cannot reach an unattended run by omission. Phase 1 tools:
docs/phase-1-plan.md:114:**MCP server (revised after the step 8 counselors review).** `McpController < ActionController::API` at `match "/mcp", via: [post, get, delete]`: a `before_action` checks the bearer (presence and byte length before `secure_compare`; never logged) and answers 401 with `WWW-Authenticate: Bearer error="invalid_token"` and a one-line JSON body, no HTML page and no `resource_metadata` (that would send Claude looking for an authorization server that does not exist). The action builds `MCP::Server` from the registry and hands the request to the gem's `StreamableHTTPTransport` with `stateless: true`, `enable_json_response: true`, `serve_subscriptions_listen: false`, `allowed_hosts` from `config.hosts` (the gem's DNS-rebinding guard rejects everything but loopback by default), and `max_request_bytes` well under the 4 MiB default; the transport handles GET, DELETE, the protocol-version header, notifications (202), and both the 2025 handshake and the 2026-07-28 stateless transport. Rails `rate_limit to: 60, within: 1.minute` on the controller; JSON only; no CORS. Tools return `MCP::Tool::Response` with `structured_content` plus a text block of the same JSON, `additionalProperties: false` on every schema, `output_schema` declared, and annotations `read_only_hint: true, destructive_hint: false, idempotent_hint: true, open_world_hint: false`. Tool exceptions never reach the transport: one dispatch wrapper rescues, writes the `tool_calls` row in an `ensure`, and returns a tool error carrying only the Schwab error message. Quotes and price history are cached with a long expiry and their own `fetched_at`; age beyond the freshness window triggers a refetch and a failed refetch returns the old entry with `stale: true`; the tools draw on a 70/min share of the Schwab budget so a chatty session cannot starve sync, and a rate limit is a tool error with `retry_after`, never a sleep. `get_portfolio` and `get_account_health` wrap the `Dashboard` read model rather than re-deriving anything; outputs carry the `protection` enum, nil for unknown risk, decimal strings for money, ISO 8601 New York timestamps, and never a `raw` column, an account hash, or the user's email (accounts are labelled by role). `get_journal` paginates (`limit` 50 default, 200 max, `offset`, `include_legs` off by default); `get_quotes` takes at most 50 symbols and reports `invalid_symbols`; `get_price_history` takes an enum range mapped to daily or weekly candles; `get_risk_rules` returns an empty list with `available: false` so the agent does not infer missing data. A spec walks every tool's serialized output for the account-hash pattern and `accountNumber`.
docs/phase-1-plan.md:116:**Connector auth.** Ship bearer-only first: in the Claude connector dialog choose Authentication "None" and add an `authorization` request header whose value is `Bearer <token>` verbatim (the headers section is an org-gated beta; header values cannot be edited after adding, so rotating the token means removing and re-adding the connector). If the dialog steers to OAuth after probing the URL, the fallback within step 8 is lazy gating: `initialize` and `tools/list` unauthenticated, the bearer required on `tools/call`. The real OAuth path (protected-resource metadata at `/.well-known/oauth-protected-resource/mcp`, authorization-server metadata, S256 PKCE, a pre-registered client id, the `https://claude.ai/api/mcp/auth_callback` redirect, a form-encoded token endpoint, refresh rotation, audience validation) is its own build step if the headers beta turns out to be unavailable, not a footnote to this one. Claude Code can drive the same server with `claude mcp add --transport http --header` for verification either way.
docs/phase-1-plan.md:204:- **Playwright** (`e2e/`, `@playwright/test` via npm, the only Node dependency): a scenarios route drawn only under `Rails.env.test?` and guarded again in the controller with `Schwab.fake?`, which resets the fake client, seeds with the factories (never by driving jobs), and signs in a seeded user by setting the session cookie because there is no inbox to read a magic link from. Times are relative: fresh is now, stale is three hours ago, expiring is 22 hours out, expired is two days ago. Scenarios: healthy, expiring, expired, halted, not connected, needs setup, one unprotected position, a failed sync with a usable token, the review queue with Skip persisting across reload, the post-mortem save, re-auth success and failure (the seeded hash must match the fake's), and the MCP 401 page (step 8). The Playwright server runs with the test queue adapter.
docs/phase-1-plan.md:227:8. MCP server + tool registry with permission metadata (after decision 0.8 is verified).
agents/counselors/1788305713-review-request-question-before-build-st/claude-fable.md:11:**3. The banner treats "never connected" as "expired."** The design branches on `SchwabToken.usable?`, which is false with no row at all. First login shows "Token expired. Sync paused. Re-authenticate," and `Sync::TickJob#pause!` has already written a pause reason that says "re-authenticate" (app/jobs/sync/tick_job.rb:49). Revoked is a third distinct case: Schwab rejected the refresh, and the copy should say so with `last_refresh_error`. `Schwab::TokenHealthJob#nag_reason` already has the right taxonomy, missing, revoked, expired, missed_sunday, expiring (app/jobs/schwab/token_health_job.rb:17-24), but it is private to the job. Extract it to `SchwabToken.status` and use it in the banner, the system page, the mailer, and `get_account_health` in step 8. That is the single method step 7 most needs and does not have.
docs/artifacts/investment-copilot-prd.md:9:A personal investment operating system for a Schwab taxable brokerage account. A Rails application acts as the system of record and the sole gateway to Schwab's Trader API. Claude acts as the analysis and workflow agent on top of it, via an MCP server the Rails app exposes. The human approves every order.
docs/artifacts/investment-copilot-prd.md:51:**Why an MCP server and not the agent hitting Schwab directly:** one credential holder, one audit log, one place guardrails run. The MCP tool surface is the contract.
docs/artifacts/investment-copilot-prd.md:159:- **Interactive (human present):** the Claude app (desktop/Cowork/web) with the Rails MCP server registered as a remote connector. Rails exposes MCP over HTTPS with bearer-token auth (token in dokku config, rotatable). This is where ad hoc analysis, screening, and order proposals happen, billed through the Claude subscription seat.
docs/artifacts/investment-copilot-prd.md:160:- **Scheduled (unattended):** Solid Queue jobs on the VPS call the Anthropic Messages API directly with an API key, passing tool definitions that map to the same internal service layer the MCP server uses (in-process, no MCP hop). Output is rendered to email. This covers the 07:00 brief, 16:30 EOD, and Sunday review. **Unattended runs get read-only tools only.** These runs ingest untrusted text (transcripts, news, fetched pages), so `propose_order`, `add_watchlist_item`, and `annotate_trade` are not in their tool set; anything a brief wants to suggest is rendered as text in the email, and the human creates the proposal interactively. Write tools exist only in the interactive runtime. Each scheduled job also has a hard cap on tool turns. Rationale: unattended runs can't depend on a desktop app being open, and keeping the scheduler in Rails means the VPS is the single ops surface (one place for cron, retries, failure alerts, and the token-health gate).
docs/artifacts/investment-copilot-prd.md:164:One tool surface, two transports: the MCP server (remote, for the app) and the API tool definitions (in-process, for jobs) both delegate to the same service objects, so guardrails and read models cannot diverge between runtimes.
