agents/counselors/1788307777-review-request-question-review-build-st/codex-5.5-high.stderr:3185:    34	  test.skip(true, "MCP lands in step 8");
agents/counselors/1788302335-review-request-question-review-build-st/codex-5.5-high.stderr:125:docs/phase-1-plan.md:18:8. **MCP auth (open verification, not blocking):** the Claude app's custom-connector UI exposes static bearer headers only to orgs in a beta. John checks Settings → Connectors → Add custom connector for a "headers" option before step 8. If absent, the fallback is a minimal OAuth 2.1 authorization-code + PKCE endpoint on the app that issues the same bearer after magic-link login. `McpAuth` verifies the bearer either way, so the tool layer is unaffected.
agents/counselors/1788302335-review-request-question-review-build-st/codex-5.5-high.stderr:451:docs/artifacts/investment-copilot-prd.md:159:- **Interactive (human present):** the Claude app (desktop/Cowork/web) with the Rails MCP server registered as a remote connector. Rails exposes MCP over HTTPS with bearer-token auth (token in dokku config, rotatable). This is where ad hoc analysis, screening, and order proposals happen, billed through the Claude subscription seat.
agents/counselors/1788302335-review-request-question-review-build-st/codex-5.5-high.stderr:452:docs/artifacts/investment-copilot-prd.md:160:- **Scheduled (unattended):** Solid Queue jobs on the VPS call the Anthropic Messages API directly with an API key, passing tool definitions that map to the same internal service layer the MCP server uses (in-process, no MCP hop). Output is rendered to email. This covers the 07:00 brief, 16:30 EOD, and Sunday review. **Unattended runs get read-only tools only.** These runs ingest untrusted text (transcripts, news, fetched pages), so `propose_order`, `add_watchlist_item`, and `annotate_trade` are not in their tool set; anything a brief wants to suggest is rendered as text in the email, and the human creates the proposal interactively. Write tools exist only in the interactive runtime. Each scheduled job also has a hard cap on tool turns. Rationale: unattended runs can't depend on a desktop app being open, and keeping the scheduler in Rails means the VPS is the single ops surface (one place for cron, retries, failure alerts, and the token-health gate).
agents/counselors/1788299995-review-request-question-review-build-st/claude-fable.md:27:- **A web request can sleep 60 seconds.** `app/models/schwab/client.rb:133` honors `Retry-After` up to the cap inside whatever thread called it. The step 8 quotes tool runs in Puma. Add a no-wait mode so web callers raise immediately and serve the cached value.
agents/counselors/1788296923-review-request-question-is-docsphase-1/claude-fable.md:13:**5. The MCP transport assumes a bearer header the Claude app may not send.** Anthropic's connector docs say static request headers for custom connectors are in beta and available to a limited set of organizations. The rest of the UI offers OAuth fields only. If John's org lacks the beta, step 8 is blocked. Verify access before step 8. If it is missing, the fallback is a minimal OAuth 2.1 authorization endpoint on the Rails app that issues the same bearer token after magic-link login. Design the token check so either path lands in the same place.
agents/counselors/1788298377-review-request-question-before-build-st/codex-5.5-high.stderr:91:A personal investment operating system for a Schwab taxable brokerage account. A Rails application acts as the system of record and the sole gateway to Schwab's Trader API. Claude acts as the analysis and workflow agent on top of it, via an MCP server the Rails app exposes. The human approves every order.
agents/counselors/1788298377-review-request-question-before-build-st/codex-5.5-high.stderr:133:**Why an MCP server and not the agent hitting Schwab directly:** one credential holder, one audit log, one place guardrails run. The MCP tool surface is the contract.
agents/counselors/1788298377-review-request-question-before-build-st/codex-5.5-high.stderr:241:- **Interactive (human present):** the Claude app (desktop/Cowork/web) with the Rails MCP server registered as a remote connector. Rails exposes MCP over HTTPS with bearer-token auth (token in dokku config, rotatable). This is where ad hoc analysis, screening, and order proposals happen, billed through the Claude subscription seat.
agents/counselors/1788298377-review-request-question-before-build-st/codex-5.5-high.stderr:242:- **Scheduled (unattended):** Solid Queue jobs on the VPS call the Anthropic Messages API directly with an API key, passing tool definitions that map to the same internal service layer the MCP server uses (in-process, no MCP hop). Output is rendered to email. This covers the 07:00 brief, 16:30 EOD, and Sunday review. **Unattended runs get read-only tools only.** These runs ingest untrusted text (transcripts, news, fetched pages), so `propose_order`, `add_watchlist_item`, and `annotate_trade` are not in their tool set; anything a brief wants to suggest is rendered as text in the email, and the human creates the proposal interactively. Write tools exist only in the interactive runtime. Each scheduled job also has a hard cap on tool turns. Rationale: unattended runs can't depend on a desktop app being open, and keeping the scheduler in Rails means the VPS is the single ops surface (one place for cron, retries, failure alerts, and the token-health gate).
agents/counselors/1788298377-review-request-question-before-build-st/codex-5.5-high.stderr:246:One tool surface, two transports: the MCP server (remote, for the app) and the API tool definitions (in-process, for jobs) both delegate to the same service objects, so guardrails and read models cannot diverge between runtimes.
agents/counselors/1788298377-review-request-question-before-build-st/codex-5.5-high.stderr:362:8. **MCP auth (open verification, not blocking):** the Claude app's custom-connector UI exposes static bearer headers only to orgs in a beta. John checks Settings → Connectors → Add custom connector for a "headers" option before step 8. If absent, the fallback is a minimal OAuth 2.1 authorization-code + PKCE endpoint on the app that issues the same bearer after magic-link login. `McpAuth` verifies the bearer either way, so the tool layer is unaffected.
agents/counselors/1788298377-review-request-question-before-build-st/codex-5.5-high.stderr:428:## 3. Tool surface (one registry, two transports)
agents/counselors/1788298377-review-request-question-before-build-st/codex-5.5-high.stderr:430:`app/models/agent/tools/*` each defines `name`, `description`, `input_schema`, `call(**args)`, and permission metadata: `read_only`, `interactive_allowed`, `scheduled_allowed`. The MCP server registers tools where `interactive_allowed`; the scheduled runtime passes only tools where `scheduled_allowed && read_only`, enforced in one place so a Phase 2 write tool cannot reach an unattended run by omission. Phase 1 tools:
agents/counselors/1788298377-review-request-question-before-build-st/codex-5.5-high.stderr:444:**MCP server:** `POST /mcp` (Streamable HTTP, JSON-RPC 2.0). `McpAuth` extracts a bearer from `Authorization` and compares with `ActiveSupport::SecurityUtils.secure_compare`; 401 renders a plain "MCP auth failed" page (Playwright target). See decision 0.8 for the OAuth fallback.
agents/counselors/1788298377-review-request-question-before-build-st/codex-5.5-high.stderr:540:8. MCP server + tool registry with permission metadata (after decision 0.8 is verified).
agents/counselors/1788298377-review-request-question-before-build-st/codex-5.5-high.stderr:604:7. MCP server (read tools only, §5.5) over HTTPS with bearer auth.
